---
import LickliderPortrait from '../components/LickliderPortrait.astro';
import {
  COMPANY_POSITION,
  MARKET_SCOPE,
  PLATFORM_SCOPE,
  PUBLIC_EVIDENCE,
} from '../data/site-facts';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const company = [
  { term: 'Company', detail: 'Licklider, Inc.' },
  { term: 'Headquarters', detail: 'N&E BLD. 7F, 1-12-4 Ginza, Chuo-ku, Tokyo, Japan' },
  { term: 'Founded', detail: 'July 2024' },
];
---

<BaseLayout title="About">
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="page-hero-inner">
      <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
      <h1>Infrastructure for the verification side of AI research</h1>
      <p class="standfirst">{COMPANY_POSITION}</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="sections">
    <section class="band">
      <p class="eyebrow">What We Do</p>
      <div class="band-body">
        <p>
          Research agents can generate analyses, code, figures, and scientific prose.
          Licklider works on the separate moment when the workflow needs to determine
          whether a bounded part of that work has an adequate, checkable basis.
        </p>
        <p>
          Our first product, nomue, begins with statistical verification. It gives a
          research agent a specialist capability for supported decisions, deterministic
          calculations, clarification requirements, refusal boundaries, and scoped
          verification results.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band">
      <p class="eyebrow">Platform</p>
      <div class="band-body">
        <p>{PLATFORM_SCOPE}</p>
        <p>{MARKET_SCOPE}</p>
        <p>
          The intended position is middleware between research agents and the evidence
          they rely on. A common verification layer can serve different agent interfaces
          and research workflows without requiring Licklider to own the general-purpose
          interface used by every researcher.
        </p>
        <p>
          Current public support remains deliberately narrow. We separate the long-term
          category, work under development, and capabilities that are publicly supported
          today.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band">
      <p class="eyebrow">Shared Language</p>
      <div class="band-body">
        <p>
          Verification is useful only when people and machines can tell what a result
          means. We publish clear distinctions between execution, clarification,
          unsupported scope, refusal, failed checks, and properties that were not
          asserted.
        </p>
        <p>
          This language is not a claim of ownership over how researchers speak. Its
          purpose is practical: help a research agent avoid silent assumptions and help a
          researcher see the exact basis and limit of an AI-assisted result.
        </p>
        <p><a href="/docs/decision-vocabulary/">Decision vocabulary →</a></p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band band--wide">
      <p class="eyebrow">How We Work</p>
      <div class="work-body">
        <div class="work-copy">
          <p>
            Precision is part of the product. We investigate very small numerical
            disagreements, build independent checks, state important non-claims, and
            keep our diagnosis separate from an upstream maintainer's confirmation.
          </p>
          <p>
            When that work finds a defect in scientific software, we report it back to
            the project. The contribution matters even when the finding never becomes a
            nomue capability.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="evidence-list">
          {
            PUBLIC_EVIDENCE.slice(0, 2).map((item) => (
              <article>
                <p class="evidence-status">{item.status}</p>
                <h2><a href={item.href}>{item.title}</a></h2>
                <p>{item.summary}</p>
              </article>
            ))
          }
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band band--wide">
      <p class="eyebrow">Why “Licklider”</p>
      <div class="why">
        <div class="band-body">
          <p>
            In “Man-Computer Symbiosis” (1960), J. C. R. Licklider described a division
            of labor in which humans set goals and exercise judgment while computers
            carry out routine work.
          </p>
          <p>
            As machines take on more generation and execution, researchers become more
            important, not less. Their judgment needs clearer evidence and better
            boundaries. Our name refers to that original vision of complementary roles.
          </p>
        </div>
        <LickliderPortrait />
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band">
      <p class="eyebrow">Company</p>
      <div class="band-body">
        <dl class="company">
          {
            company.map((row) => (
              <>
                <dt>{row.term}</dt>
                <dd>{row.detail}</dd>
              </>
            ))
          }
        </dl>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .page-hero { border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule-faint); }
  .page-hero-inner { max-width: var(--shell); margin: 0 auto; padding: 5.5rem var(--gutter) 4.5rem; }
  .page-hero h1 { margin: 1.1rem 0 1.75rem; font-size: clamp(2.2rem, 4.8vw, 3.4rem); line-height: 1.2; letter-spacing: -0.01em; }
  .standfirst { max-width: var(--measure); margin: 0; font-size: 1.08rem; color: var(--ink-2); }
  .sections { max-width: var(--shell); margin: 0 auto; padding: 0 var(--gutter); }
  .band { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 12rem minmax(0, 1fr); gap: 3rem; padding: 4rem 0; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule-faint); }
  .band:last-child { border-bottom: 0; }
  .band > .eyebrow { margin: 0.35rem 0 0; }
  .band-body { max-width: var(--measure); }
  .band-body > :last-child, article > :last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }
  .work-body { max-width: 52rem; }
  .work-copy { max-width: var(--measure); }
  .evidence-list { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: 1rem; margin-top: 2rem; }
  .evidence-list article { padding: 1.25rem; border: 1px solid var(--rule); }
  .evidence-list h2 { margin-bottom: 0.65rem; font-size: 1.02rem; }
  .evidence-status { margin-bottom: 0.5rem; color: var(--ink-3); font-size: 0.72rem; letter-spacing: 0.08em; text-transform: uppercase; }
  .why { display: grid; grid-template-columns: minmax(0, var(--measure)) 10rem; gap: 4rem; align-items: start; }
  .company { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 9rem minmax(0, 1fr); row-gap: 0.5rem; margin: 0; font-size: 0.95rem; }
  .company dt { padding-top: 0.15rem; color: var(--ink-3); font-size: 0.85rem; }
  .company dd { margin: 0; }

  @media (max-width: 800px) {
    .page-hero-inner { padding: 3.5rem var(--gutter) 3rem; }
    .band { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); gap: 1.25rem; padding: 3rem 0; }
    .why { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); gap: 2rem; }
    .company { grid-template-columns: 7.5rem minmax(0, 1fr); }
  }

  @media (max-width: 620px) {
    .evidence-list { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }
  }
</style>
